WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.610
- Good afternoon everybody.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.850
I'm Gary Tomlinson,

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.960
the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.860
Not for much longer, the incoming director

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.693
is Alice Kaplan

00:00:16.693 --> 00:00:17.936
and she is right down there.

00:00:17.936 --> 00:00:18.769
(laughing)

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:20.500
Couldn't resist pointing that out Alice.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.500
But I want to welcome you
to the Finzi-Contini lecture

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.340
for this semester by
distinguished journalist

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.100
and writer Philippe Lancon,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.790
it promises to be a wonderful

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.563
and inspirational presentation.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.770
Before I turn the podium over

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.320
to the introducer of our speaker,

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.440
we do this seriatim,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.870
I'd like to say a few preliminary words.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.340
The Finzi-Contini lectureship
was endowed in 1990

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.190
by the honorable Guido Calabresi,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.370
judge of the U.S. Court of
Appeal for the Second Circuit

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.020
and former Dean of the Yale Law School

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.330
and his brother, Doctor Paul Calabresi

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.310
in memory of their mother,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.263
Bianca Maria Finzi-Contini Calabresi.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.320
She was a scholar of European literature

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.010
and a native of Ferrara.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:07.370
She fled fascism in Italy
along with her husband,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.810
Doctor Massimo Calabresi

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.330
and settled here in New Haven.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.260
Guido Calabresi just
presented me a moment ago

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.380
with a photograph.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.310
He's about five years
old in the photograph

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.990
and there they are in New Haven already.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.440
It's an extraordinary photograph.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.050
The whole family with Bianca Maria

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.300
up at the top of the steps.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.410
In any case she was a scholar
of European literature

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.760
native of the Ferrara.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:27.970
They settled here in New Haven,

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.660
she earned a PhD in French at Yale

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.120
with a dissertation on French

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.493
and Breton historian Ernest Renan.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.640
She became professor of French and Italian

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.930
at Connecticut College,

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.590
then was for many years
professor and chair

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.830
of the Department of Italian
at Albertus Magnus College.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.870
She died in 1982 at the age of 80.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.540
Guido Calabresi and his
wife Ann are here today.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.880
They're right down here in the second row

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.580
and I'd like you to
join me in thanking them

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.543
and their family for their generosity.

00:01:55.543 --> 00:01:58.460
(crowd applauding)

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:09.160
They remain extraordinarily
engaged and busy busy people

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.910
and it's always wonderful to see them here

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.433
in the Whitney Humanities Center audience.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.900
After the lecture there will
be a reception in room 108

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:19.450
which is straight down
the hall behind you.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.660
Most of you know where that
reception room is already.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.370
You're all invited to it. Please do come.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.930
And last but not least,

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.790
I'm directed by Yale legal counsel

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.310
to read the following paragraph.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.900
Our events may be recorded

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.470
or live streamed or photographed

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.340
for educational, archival
or promotional purposes

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.170
including use in print on the internet

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.290
and in other forms of media.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.780
All attendees and participants

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.430
or parents or guardians of
minors attending the event agree

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.620
by virtue of their attending the event

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.590
or otherwise participating in the event

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.130
to the possibility of their voices

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.460
or likenesses being
captured by these means

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:55.960
and used for such purposes

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.780
without compensation to them.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.210
And they hereby waive any
related right of privacy

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.333
or publicity.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.070
Such as the world we live in.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.220
That's not part of the announcement

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.400
but in any case, yes
you are duly announced.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.770
(giggles)

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:13.920
Introducing Philippe Lancon
today will be Morgane Cadieu,

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.890
assistant professor of French here at Yale

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.140
and it's my great pleasure to welcome

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:18.590
to the podium Professor Cadieu

00:03:19.713 --> 00:03:22.880
(audience applauding)

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.533
- Good afternoon.

00:03:28.533 --> 00:03:31.430
Welcome to the 2020 Finzi-Contini lecture

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.070
and thank you very much
for adding this conference

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:35.190
to your schedule.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.590
So my name is Morgane Cadieu.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.750
I'm an associate professor
in the Department of French,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.810
specializing in contemporary fiction

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.900
and experimental literature.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.530
I'd like to start by thanking

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.660
the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.810
Gary Tomlinson

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.840
and the chair of the
department of French Alice Kaplan

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.290
for making this even possible

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.810
and for giving me the honor

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.613
of introducing our guest this afternoon.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.870
We're delighted to welcome Phillip Lancon,

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.710
a French writer, journalist
and cultural critic

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.250
specialized in French and
Latin American literatures.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.180
He studied at Duke University

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.250
and lectured at Princeton
University on literature,

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.080
culture society and violence.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.610
Named the Chevalier du le
Ordre des Arts et des Lettres

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.890
by the French government,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:20.887
he's also the 2012 recipient of the

00:04:20.887 --> 00:04:23.470
(speaking foreign language)

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.010
from the French Academy,

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:28.730
a prize aimed at supporting
literary creation.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:30.520
In 2013, he was recognized

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.100
with the Jean-Luc Lagardere Award

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.290
for journalist of the year.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.090
He writes for the daily
newspaper Liberation,

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:38.770
the magazine Vontielle

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:43.210
and for the satirical weekly
newspaper, Charlie Hebdo.

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.150
In January 7, 2015

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.630
Philippe Lancon was seriously wounded

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.050
during the terrorist attack perpetrated

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:51.680
against Charlie Hebdo.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.580
A shooting that killed 12 persons

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.973
and wounded 11.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.330
He spent nine months in
the Parisian hospitals,

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.640
Le pitie Salpetriere and Les Invalides,

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.310
the great monument which still serves

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.373
as a hospital for veterans.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.210
His memoir Le Lambeau,

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.190
published by Gallimard in 2018

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:11.210
recounts the attack

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.300
but his first and foremost is a narrative

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.580
about the body,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.150
about how the event

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:19.560
and injuries affected
the author's relationship

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.700
to his parents, friends and lovers.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.920
But also to painting music and literature.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.580
It's a book about what it means

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:31.140
to be guarded by police officers 24 seven,

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.110
a hospital diary

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.430
and the split between the patient

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.633
and what the author calls l'homme d'avan,

00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:38.193
the man from before.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.410
The narrator of Le Lambeau

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.690
details his new experience of space

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.580
which he calls the grammar
of the hospital room.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:46.727
I quote,

00:05:46.727 --> 00:05:49.737
"Those who entered the room had out sided

00:05:49.737 --> 00:05:52.747
"less existence than
characters in the novel

00:05:52.747 --> 00:05:54.760
"once the book was closed."

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.260
end of quote.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.120
Le Lambeau is also reflection on time.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.080
Although the narrator is an
avid reader of Marcel Proust,

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.410
he explicitly rejects the
expression, "time regained"

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.540
which is the title of the last volume

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.500
of À la recherche du temps perdu

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:12.010
To capture the singularity of his recovery

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.920
he favors expressions such as,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.410
time interrupted,

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.853
mixed time and suspended time.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.700
In the aftermath of the 2015 attack,

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:22.604
many started to use the slogan

00:06:22.604 --> 00:06:24.840
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.470
notably during demonstrations
against terrorism

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.620
and for the freedom of the press.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.030
Philippe Lancon was in
the hospital at the time

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.040
and was as he explains it,

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.111
not charlie but Chloe.

00:06:36.111 --> 00:06:38.510
Le Lambeau, he writes Jessie Chloe

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.460
which is the first name of his surgeon

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.530
Doctor Chloe Bertolus,

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.400
head of the Maxillofacial Surgery Service

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.710
at the Pitie Lepetriere and
a key character in his book.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.350
The French title Le Lambeau
refers to a specific type

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.770
of reconstructive surgery
called the fibula flap.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:59.690
In French Le Lambeau also means a bit,

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:00.638
a scrap, a shred

00:07:00.638 --> 00:07:01.471
or a tatter.

00:07:01.471 --> 00:07:05.800
Last fall Europa editions
published the English version

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.280
of Le Lambeau translated
by Stephen Randall

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.483
as Disturbance, Surviving Charlie Hebdo.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:14.920
Le Lambeau is a best-seller
anonymously acclaimed

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:18.380
by critics and the winner of many prizes

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.270
the Prix Feemina, the Prix du Roman News,

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.603
the Jury's special selection
of the Prix Renaudot,

00:07:24.603 --> 00:07:25.436
(speaks in foreign language)

00:07:25.436 --> 00:07:26.580
awarded yearly

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.430
to both the Latin american writer

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:30.710
and to a French writer.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.520
Le Lambeau was named Best Book of the Year

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.858
by the magazine's Lire
and Les Inrockuptibles.

00:07:35.858 --> 00:07:38.610
Philippe Lancon is also
the author of two novels,

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.750
Lis iles in 2011,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.890
the islands of the title
be Hong Kong in Cuba

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:46.030
and L'Elan on the urge

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:49.050
or the impetus in English in 2013

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.133
centered on

00:07:50.133 --> 00:07:50.966
(mumbles)

00:07:50.966 --> 00:07:54.400
And one can not is that
his writings all rhyme.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.733
Les iles, L'Elan, Le Lambeau.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.887
His lecture today starts with a question,

00:07:59.887 --> 00:08:01.950
"Did I write a feel-good book?"

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.410
So, please welcome our guest

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:05.063
Philip Lancon very warmly.

00:08:06.393 --> 00:08:09.476
(audience applauding)

00:08:25.412 --> 00:08:26.500
- Thank you. Good evening.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.370
Thank you to be here.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:29.203
So,

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.037
as my title is,

00:08:33.037 --> 00:08:34.380
"Did I write a feel-good book?"

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.750
And the purpose of this
little conference is

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.813
to answer it or not to answer it.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.800
Before Christmas,

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.230
a French literary magazine asks me

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:50.690
to draw up a list of my
favorite feel-good books.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.033
Obviously I was not the only writer

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:55.253
whom the magazine asked to do this.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:00.023
It was to be part of a
feature on feel-good books.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.210
When the end-of-the-year
holidays approach,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.320
everything becomes depressing.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.840
And we want everyone to be happy.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:12.470
So we give them chocolate
or feel-good books.

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:15.240
Sometimes we obtain a result opposite

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.290
to the one we were hoping for.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:19.810
Indigestion in the case of chocolates

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:23.040
and the Blues in the
case of feel-good books.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:26.120
Maybe that's what we were after.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:28.160
We don't necessarily like the people

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.960
to whom we think we are
obliged to give presents

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.730
or we don't like them well

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.870
giving them the gifts that
we would like to receive.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:36.880
The problem is that

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.490
even aside from the question of taste,

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.253
we are never in their shoes.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:45.180
I suppose some writers would have liked

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.640
to put their own books
at the top of the list

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.623
but of course no one did that.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.600
Also writers are almost always convinced

00:09:55.474 --> 00:09:57.720
that they are talent,

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.703
they know how to find
modesty when they have to.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:02.553
However,

00:10:02.553 --> 00:10:03.897
I said to myself,

00:10:03.897 --> 00:10:05.757
"Would they really have wanted their books

00:10:05.757 --> 00:10:08.087
"to be at the top of
the list they drew up?"

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.500
I don't know many writers would feel proud

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.220
of having written a feel-good book.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.260
It would seem to most of them

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.490
that they were acting like priests,

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.940
psychotherapists,

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.723
or simply charlatans.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.070
In one in one way or another,

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:28.580
a writer claims to be talking
about what people experience,

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:30.790
think and feel.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:35.600
This reality isn't intended
to make us feel good.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:38.560
It isn't intended for anything

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:40.310
either good or bad.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.900
It is content to be born, grow

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.000
and flow in every way possible like water.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.110
Sometimes our imagination gives it

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.610
banks, dikes or dams

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:56.210
but it immediately overflows them.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.640
Often this reality circulates in a hole.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:02.560
We were peacefully
skating on a frozen lake

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:04.160
looking out of the sky

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.680
like a heroine in a Russian novel

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.340
or a little figure in a Bragger painting.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:11.650
Suddenly, the ice cracks

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.083
and we fall into the hole.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:16.100
This was not foreseen.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:17.830
That's what happened to me.

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:22.360
Below the water is cold
and you can't see anything.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.660
At the very bottom there are huge monsters

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:27.830
that never see the light of day.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:29.110
You would touch them,

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:30.620
you feel them,

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.380
you fear them,

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.060
you don't tame them.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:36.610
You don't know exactly
what they look like.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:39.820
But it's possible to
describe the perceptions,

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:41.060
sensations

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:42.270
and feelings

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:44.340
that they elicit in us.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.033
That they impose on us.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:48.950
As a reader,

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:52.340
I initially found the
magazine requests ridiculous.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:54.070
But since I'm a journalist,

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:55.060
I wasn't surprised

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:58.430
because I know how most
periodicals operate.

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:01.350
They love to make lists and reward prizes

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.410
especially as I have said,

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.620
at the end of the year.

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:09.500
You would think their editors
had never left school.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.180
I imagine they must
have been model students

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:14.350
or they want to think their readers we're

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:16.170
like them model students.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:19.870
The ones who were rewarded in my time

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:23.550
at least with fine books
at the end of the year.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.920
Even if the reports in the newspaper

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.837
become the lives of losers,

00:12:29.651 --> 00:12:32.700
the lives of those will
never appear on any

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:34.790
and enviable list.

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:37.290
They themselves live in
the world of winners,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:40.070
the one of people were on the good list

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:40.983
and they know it.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.340
And they never stop doing what winners do,

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:47.620
lecturing losers on what they have to do.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:51.003
Feel-good books are
part of this lecturing.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:58.540
If this magazine's request annoyed me,

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:01.380
it was so because my first question was,

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.003
why ask me that.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.810
Was it by any chance because they thought

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:09.550
that Disturbance was a feel-good book?

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.380
I began my reply to the journalist

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.300
who had written me by saying

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.120
that I would ask my physician

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.480
to draw up this list in my place.

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:19.440
Because after all,

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:24.440
what they were asking
for was a prescription.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.540
Nothing is easier for me than
to obtain a prescription.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:32.630
Since the attack, I have been
surrounded by physicians.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:35.490
Like more years imaginary invalid.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:37.760
If one of them isn't the available

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.690
to write a prescription for me,

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.050
another will do it.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.710
My physicians don't refuse me anything.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:46.180
They are attentive,

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.330
generous.

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:48.310
In exchange,

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:51.230
they get my discipline and my gratitude.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.700
That I had it.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:54.930
You know how they are.

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:58.193
At least in France no one
can read their writing.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.540
You would think they write
like certain authors do

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:04.293
so as not to be read.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:09.160
As if they preferred
obscurity to misunderstanding.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.440
when the patient enters our office is

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.090
because he feels sick.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:15.370
When he comes out and
read the prescription

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:17.480
before going to the pharmacy,

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:20.080
he feels stupid or illiterate.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:24.750
Fortunately the pharmacist
is used to the physician

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:28.230
and knows how to decipher
his hieroglyphics

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.030
So I told the journalist

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.140
that if he wanted to decipher the list

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.920
of feel good books drawn
up by my physician,

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.900
you would probably need the help

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:40.530
of an expert in physiology
or a pharmacist.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:42.040
And I said to myself,

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:44.280
saved. They won't persist.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.420
But since I am a journalist,

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:49.600
I should have known what
most periodicals are like.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.980
My correspondent immediately replied

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:53.290
that he liked my answer

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:56.700
and would be delighted to
have me develop it freely

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.453
but please no more than 300 words.

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:02.000
He was right.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.870
Prescriptions are supposed to be short.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:08.133
Too much medicine leads to intoxication.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:14.030
But what feel-good books,

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:17.460
was I going to ask my
doctor to put on the list?

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.450
And which of my doctor should I ask?

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:22.600
My personal surgeon,

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:25.060
the other surgeons who treated me,

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:27.780
one of my physical therapist?

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:31.440
At the hospital in January 2015,

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.920
my first physical therapist

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:39.230
had advised me to read Tom
McNab's Flanagan's run.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:40.850
This is a story of a man

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:44.840
who during The Great Depression
organized a race on foot

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:46.650
across the United State.

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:48.950
Despite difficulties and setbacks,

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:52.283
several competitors came
to know one another,

00:15:53.375 --> 00:15:54.700
like one another,

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:56.160
how to do one another,

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:58.240
everything hard ends well.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.230
I quote a summary

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.940
because I never read the book.

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:03.550
If I understand correctly,

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:06.250
this book is an optimistic counterpart

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:08.217
of Horace McCoy's novel,

00:16:08.217 --> 00:16:10.270
"They Shoot Horses, Don't They?"

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:13.120
Where everything hard ends badly.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.120
This novel, I did read.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.870
I also saw the film that
Sydney Pollack made of it

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:19.510
starring Jane Fonda.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:23.010
But I didn't watch it
all the way to the end.

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:25.767
I must have been 10 or 12 years old

00:16:25.767 --> 00:16:29.420
and my parents sent me to bed around 9:30.

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:32.376
The next morning I asked
my mother to tell me

00:16:32.376 --> 00:16:34.060
how the film ended

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:37.460
and she told me that Jane Fonda died.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:38.990
I was traumatized

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:41.383
and to this day I've
never seen the film again.

00:16:42.547 --> 00:16:47.547
The novel by McNab, a former athlete

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.020
dates from 1982

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:52.830
that of Michael McCoy,
a former journalist,

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:54.710
from 1935.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.750
I imagine that the first novel,

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.380
the one I didn't read
uses The Great Depression

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:04.340
as a historical framework
in which the experience

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:07.240
and optimistic message of the athlete,

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:09.640
the winner can be developed.

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:13.230
In the second novel
there is no such message.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:18.230
The Great Depression is
not a historical framework.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:23.100
McCoy experienced and
underwent it directly.

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:25.910
Writers who have been on the losers' side

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:30.310
that is on the side of
an excess of reality

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:32.340
don't write feel-good books.

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:35.683
They know that Jane Fonda dies at the end.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:38.580
Three years later,

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:42.020
the same physical therapist
resumed treating me

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:44.040
again in her office.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:47.120
In the meantime I had
started running again

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:49.200
despite my missing fibula,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.610
the one that now serves
as my lower jawbone.

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:54.600
The physical therapist told me

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.750
that she also run with her dog,

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:59.910
a Cocker Spaniel named Happy.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:02.230
One of the first question she asked me was

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:04.860
"Have you read Flanagan's run?"

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:06.020
I almost say yes

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:09.120
but I was afraid she
would ask me to elaborate,

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:11.450
tell her what I thought of the novel

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:14.170
while she was massaging my cheeks and jaw.

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:15.870
So I preferred to tell the truth

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:19.610
and I said I still hadn't bought it.

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:22.380
At my next visit she gave me a copy.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:25.910
She regularly asks me
whether I've read it.

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:27.490
It's in my library

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.080
between the books of Thomas McGuane

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:32.590
and those of Robert McLiam Wilson.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:34.680
Two very pessimistic novelists

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:35.970
that I have read.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:38.410
But I still haven't opened Flanagan's Run.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:42.080
So, I couldn't put it on the list

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.170
that the magazine was asking me for.

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:47.310
Which book should I put on the list?

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:48.910
To answer that question,

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:52.730
I first had to ask
another more specific one

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:56.070
to which I've just began to reply.

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:58.740
What is a feel-good book?

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:02.650
It wasn't possible to answer
that question in 300 words

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.970
so in my response to the magazine,

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:06.870
I wrote about other subjects

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:08.380
including a writer I like

00:19:09.482 --> 00:19:12.840
who so far as I know never
what a feel-good book

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:14.870
but whose works have

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.723
nonetheless greatly benefited me, Nabokov.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:23.410
Before continuing my response
here in more than 300 words,

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:27.200
I have to admit as you
have no doubt understood

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:29.203
that I don't like lists.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:32.260
They always seem to be intended

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:34.310
to empower those who make then

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.420
and to exclude or make uncomfortable

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:39.360
those were not on them.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:42.650
They remind me of our ceremonies at school

00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:45.700
and even of the smell of
the cafeteria of the school

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.750
another of cauliflower and bechamel sauce.

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:51.670
But not being the editor of a newspaper

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:54.530
I have never dreamed of awarding prizes

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.800
and I have no nostalgia about school

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:58.770
not even olfactif.

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:00.873
My memories of the classroom are

00:20:00.873 --> 00:20:02.330
neither good nor bad.

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.770
They are simply mediocre and gray

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.310
like the student that I rapidly became

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:08.820
after middle school.

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:13.340
I was particularly fond of
dancers and troublemakers.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:15.680
The ones who didn't make me feel good,

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:17.770
the feel bad guys.

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:21.060
I didn't understand why
I wasn't very smart.

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:22.610
But it seemed to me

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:24.540
that truth was on their side

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:26.550
on the side of the discomfort

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:30.250
that they spread around
themselves and in me.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:33.400
This discomfort was not without charm,

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:34.870
without danger

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:36.883
and without loneliness.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:42.050
It led me to do stupid
things to is isolate myself

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:46.470
to have disagreeable
and unpredictable moods

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:49.310
that were unexpected even for me.

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:52.990
And also to expose myself
to all kinds of reporters

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.863
first of all my own.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:57.970
I believe that I truly began to read

00:20:57.970 --> 00:20:59.840
after this discomfort

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:03.600
and in the loneliness
to which it gave rise.

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:06.480
It was this discomfort that helped me

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:09.660
to understand Rimbaud, Proust, Kafka

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:11.850
and quite a few other writers.

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:14.770
That is how my feel-good books came to be.

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:17.480
There are books that have allowed me

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:21.870
to overcome discomfort by exploring it.

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:24.610
They have been my unknown friends.

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:27.890
These unknown friends were guides

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:30.040
like Sergeant Tyree,

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:32.667
the army scoot in Indian Territory in

00:21:32.667 --> 00:21:34.460
"She Wore a Yellow Ribbon",

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:35.803
John Ford's western.

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.050
Sergeant Tyree is young

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:42.110
but no longer as young as he used to be.

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:44.530
He is courageous, ironic,

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.060
always reserved.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:48.880
He's a contrary of a Miles Gloriosus,

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:51.530
a know-it-all or a blabbermouth.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.580
He's a former Confederate soldier

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:57.780
who now works without
scruples or hesitations

00:21:57.780 --> 00:21:59.130
for the Federal Army.

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:01.430
That is his former enemies

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:04.220
because this was the
South lost the civil War.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:05.860
When I was a child

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.010
I preferred the Confederate.

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:10.110
I didn't know anything about slavery

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:12.020
or the deep causes of the war.

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:13.900
If I liked the Confederate,

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.290
it was because they inspired rich films.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:18.290
Films that made me dream.

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:20.720
And precisely because they lost.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:22.380
I liked the Confederates

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:24.100
and I like the Indians.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:25.710
Without realizing it,

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:27.280
I liked the losers.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:31.070
They dreamed and they had
an identity like the winners

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.910
but they were dispossessed of their dreams

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:35.470
and of their identity.

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:38.990
They had tried out all
the possibilities of life.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:42.100
The worst as well as the best.

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:45.600
Sergeant Tyree no longer
has any illusions.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:47.420
He doesn't show off

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:52.330
that he has experience and
he has a sense of duty.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:54.730
He has the lucidity and
the distance peculiar

00:22:54.730 --> 00:22:56.450
to certain phantoms

00:22:56.450 --> 00:22:58.250
who are detached from everything

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:01.210
except the job they have to do.

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:02.550
He doesn't have the violence

00:23:02.550 --> 00:23:05.050
and bitterness of Ethan Edwards,

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:07.990
the role played by John
Wayne in "The Searchers".

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:09.380
He seems to have understood

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:11.400
that the best we can do is

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:13.560
to remain in the modest position

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:16.593
that work and talent have
allowed us to obtain.

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:22.290
He goes off all alone to
explore the Indian territories,

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:23.123
unknown,

00:23:23.123 --> 00:23:24.170
threatening,

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:25.240
marvelous,

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.180
hostile lands,

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.430
the territories of Death Valley.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:32.160
And he comes back with
information that he gives

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:35.480
without commentary to
Captain Nathan Brittles,

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.500
also played by John Wayne.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:40.150
When he is asked what he thinks

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:41.220
of what he has seen,

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:43.490
he smiled slightly

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:44.637
and answers,

00:23:44.637 --> 00:23:46.627
"That aint my department, sir."

00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:50.740
Naturally Captain Brittles knows as we do

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:52.750
what Sergeant Tyree thinks

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:54.600
and even that he is right

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:55.770
because is the first.

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:58.290
The only person who has seen the reality

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:00.430
of life in this territory.

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:02.970
And because he is intelligent

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:07.450
but except for one time when
the captain gets irritated

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:11.550
he offers no interpretation
of what he has observed.

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:13.160
He is not afraid,

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:14.470
he doesn't complain

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:15.730
or comment

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:17.130
or judge.

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:20.210
Neither does he say what he escaped.

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:22.510
He returns impact Babel

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:26.680
from the enchanted and
funeral land of Death Valley.

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:30.610
I dream of being able to
conclude each of my stories

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:32.480
at the moment when a judgement,

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:34.900
an interpretation is expected

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.177
with these words,

00:24:36.177 --> 00:24:39.060
"That ain't my department, sir."

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:42.460
The feel-good books in my life have

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:44.630
almost all been Sergeant Tyree's

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:47.590
but I understood that only when

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:49.860
myself returned from Death Valley

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.460
with a shattered jaw bone

00:24:51.460 --> 00:24:53.580
and with an heck wounded hands

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:56.220
riding behind the Herod with my hands

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:58.420
around his waist like a child

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:00.710
so I wouldn't fall.

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:03.200
My feel-good books written as he does

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.550
now with me from unknown,

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:06.630
threatening,

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:07.640
marvelous

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:09.193
and hostile lands.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:14.300
Sometimes they give me
information with a slight smile,

00:25:14.300 --> 00:25:16.010
Something precise and mysterious

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:17.850
that makes me want to go to

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:20.190
or return from the place

00:25:21.538 --> 00:25:25.640
from which they come whatever the cost.

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:29.090
Sometimes they find me and save me

00:25:29.090 --> 00:25:30.900
when I'm wounded,

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:32.620
lost in the middle of the desert

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:35.220
and surrounded by hostile Indians.

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:37.840
As those who have read Disturbance know

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:40.347
I had essentially Three Sergeant Tyrees

00:25:40.347 --> 00:25:44.040
during my nine months of
adventures in hospitals.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.600
Thomas Mann, "Magic Mountain",

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:48.400
Kafka's "Letters to Milena"

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.810
and Marcel Proust, "In
Search of Lost Time".

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.320
They were scouts or saviors

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:56.520
sometimes both at once.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:26:00.510
At no time did they give
me certainties or receives.

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:02.560
They gave me neither good news

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:04.080
nor a happy end.

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:06.620
They held up countless mirrors

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:08.030
to my own life.

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:11.330
But unlike Rita Hayworth
in "The Lady from Shanghai"

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:14.300
I don't think I shattered
in their reflections.

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.710
On the contrary they allowed me

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:19.135
to detach and concentrate myself.

00:26:19.135 --> 00:26:24.123
They helped make me in part
at least Sergeant Tyree.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:29.300
Of course I'm aware that most people

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:31.240
especially here in America

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:33.400
a feel-good book is
something very different

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:36.690
from the silhouette and
taciturn personality

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:38.210
of Sergeant Tyree.

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:41.300
Usually, it is a book of good will.

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:44.070
A book of initiatory propaganda.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:45.670
It recounts a test,

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:47.240
physical or moral

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:50.660
that has been undergone
and overcome by the author

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:52.760
who comes out of it stronger,

00:26:52.760 --> 00:26:55.080
happier and more intelligent.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:59.580
Or in any case who
presents himself that way.

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:00.820
At the same time,

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:03.690
it gives its distressed readers

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:06.420
a few tips on how to cope.

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.820
These tips are easy to follow,

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:11.100
a choice guide to survival.

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:14.750
There is no feel-good
book without a happy end.

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:17.840
It's Flanagan's run written by a coach.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:20.160
At the end of the book

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:21.610
as at the end of a play

00:27:21.610 --> 00:27:24.350
the coach returns to
the stage one last time

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:25.740
to thank his wife, children,

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:26.960
friends, publisher,

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:28.140
grandmother, cousins,

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:29.340
colleagues, country

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:30.700
and even God

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.240
without whom he would not be

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:34.700
today's Marvel's person he has become.

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:38.720
And whom success along
with his customers awaits.

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:43.220
In short it's a fairy
tale set in a gymnasium.

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:47.360
When I was in the hospital
my companion Gabriela

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:50.870
absurdly wanted me to
read this kind of book

00:27:50.870 --> 00:27:52.440
because that's what she did

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:54.600
when she wasn't feeling well.

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:56.470
She didn't understand how I could read

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:57.317
for example,

00:27:57.317 --> 00:27:59.680
"In Search of Lost Time".

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:01.430
Two years before the attack,

00:28:01.430 --> 00:28:04.100
I had read passages from the novel to her.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.290
Passages that were quite funny

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:10.010
to amuse her and make her
feel the power of style

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:12.250
and the absurdity of life.

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:16.710
She didn't understand what
pleasure one could take

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:18.550
in reading about solitude,

00:28:18.550 --> 00:28:19.750
misunderstandings,

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:21.800
jealousies, lies, sufferings

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:25.840
and nasty unhappy and
ridiculous characters.

00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:27.570
I didn't keep trying

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:29.850
what would that do.

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:33.420
Gabriela from Chile but she
had been living in New York

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:34.930
for such a long time

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:36.550
that she seemed to me

00:28:36.550 --> 00:28:40.320
to have lost the vital sense
of irony and melancholy

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:41.730
in such a hot city

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:43.890
where everyone seems
to think it is possible

00:28:43.890 --> 00:28:45.350
in spite of everything

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:47.940
to survive and to survive and win.

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:52.160
Real feel-good books are
probably a necessity.

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:55.790
So she gave me several
several of them, all American.

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:57.570
She arranged even for me

00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:59.380
to meet the author of one of them.

00:28:59.380 --> 00:29:02.540
A man who had been badly burned.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:05.920
We had breakfast with him
in a cafe in North Carolina.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:07.330
I had ordered an omelette

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:10.380
because it was easier for
me to eat at this time.

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:13.200
Nevertheless, bits of egg were falling

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.067
on my trousers as he talked.

00:29:15.067 --> 00:29:17.360
"The future belongs to us you know."

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:19.670
I wiped my chin and forgot it all.

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:22.410
Gabriela also sent me links to videos

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:23.243
in which a woman

00:29:23.243 --> 00:29:25.760
who was very famous in your country told

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:29.170
how she had survived some
deadly illness or other.

00:29:29.170 --> 00:29:30.250
Was it an accident?

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:31.930
I no longer remember.

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:33.490
I've forgotten her name.

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:37.180
Her act was excellent
and for me terrifying.

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:39.770
It was a kind of stand-up feel-good.

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:41.640
I don't like stand-up.

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:44.900
I had only to read a few
pages of these books,

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:46.640
watch the few minutes of these videos

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.530
to feel absolutely depressed.

00:29:48.530 --> 00:29:50.570
For me there is nothing worse

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:53.050
when one has been the victim of an illness

00:29:53.050 --> 00:29:54.280
or a serious trauma

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:58.050
than reading or listening
to survivors tell you

00:29:58.050 --> 00:29:59.707
with a cheerful air,

00:29:59.707 --> 00:30:01.077
"Do as I did.

00:30:01.077 --> 00:30:03.250
"You'll get over it."

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:07.480
I simply felt like tearing up the book

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:09.190
unplugging my computer,

00:30:09.190 --> 00:30:10.680
killing the couches

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:13.730
or disappearing for example into a forest.

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:15.410
Not only did these people

00:30:15.410 --> 00:30:16.860
with their self-satisfaction,

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:18.840
their superficial optimism

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:20.720
and their instruction manuals

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:24.980
not help me understand
what I was experiencing.

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:28.510
But I felt that if I spend
too much time with them

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:31.550
I would become definitely stupid.

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:33.740
Since I'm a little corny,

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:37.890
I waited until Gabriela
and I have separated

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:39.550
to throw away these books.

00:30:39.550 --> 00:30:43.410
I didn't throw them away
immediately after our separation.

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:45.310
For a year and a half,

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.180
they remained on my bookshelf

00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:48.230
like foils

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:50.160
or perhaps talismans

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.310
that I didn't dare touch.

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:56.010
I think I was afraid they
might take vengeance on me.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:57.670
You didn't believe in us

00:30:57.670 --> 00:30:59.973
so you are going to fail.

00:30:59.973 --> 00:31:03.860
There's always a threat in
this kind of feel-good books.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:06.287
If you don't believe in me they murmur,

00:31:06.287 --> 00:31:07.337
"You're lost."

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:12.180
As there other definitions
of feel-good books.

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.473
Take for example Madame Bovary.

00:31:16.570 --> 00:31:19.000
Emma's use is narrated by Flaubert

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:21.563
at the beginning of chapter six

00:31:22.783 --> 00:31:24.500
of the first part of the novel.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:26.410
Flaubert tells us about the characters

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:28.860
with whom the young woman identified

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:30.410
with whom she felt in accord

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:32.750
and grew up in a convent

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:35.680
and in the purest romantic tradition.

00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:37.180
They were born in a best-seller

00:31:37.180 --> 00:31:39.820
of the end of the 18th century

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:41.330
and the beginning of the nineteenth

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:43.730
published one year before
the French Revolution.

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:46.427
She had read quote,

00:31:46.427 --> 00:31:48.457
"She had read Poland Virginia

00:31:48.457 --> 00:31:51.127
"and she had dreamed about
the little bamboo house,

00:31:51.127 --> 00:31:52.497
"the nigger Domingo,

00:31:52.497 --> 00:31:53.797
"the dog Fidel,

00:31:53.797 --> 00:31:56.747
"but above all about the sweet friendship

00:31:56.747 --> 00:31:58.787
"of some dear little brother

00:31:58.787 --> 00:32:03.787
"who seeks red fruit for you
on trees taller than steeples

00:32:03.937 --> 00:32:06.277
"or who runs barefoot over the sand

00:32:06.277 --> 00:32:09.010
"bringing you a bird's nest."

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:11.480
Flaubert is ironic as usual

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:13.760
and maybe even sarcastic.

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:16.426
In is work there is almost always

00:32:16.426 --> 00:32:19.530
because of the sculpture of his sentences

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:22.770
an agitation between distance and wonder

00:32:22.770 --> 00:32:25.053
between beauty and reality.

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:29.960
But in these lines it is
precisely the primary effect

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:31.320
of a feel-good book

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:34.020
as I conceive it that one reads.

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:35.330
It's the reverie

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.520
that brings the solitary reader closer

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:40.840
to the characters he is discovering

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:43.240
ends up creating a third person.

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:46.560
A person who is neither entirely real

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:49.002
nor entirely fictional.

00:32:49.002 --> 00:32:52.150
A person who like a patient under morphine

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:55.630
calms the pain of existence

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:59.780
and intoxicates herself at the same time.

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:02.980
A person who if she's defenseless

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:07.450
and without information about
the territory she's entering,

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:10.330
about the world in which she lives,

00:33:10.330 --> 00:33:14.280
if she has not acquired
Sergeant Tyree's point of view

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:17.393
will inevitably be massacred.

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:22.990
Unlike Emma Bovary I
did not go to a convent

00:33:22.990 --> 00:33:25.620
and I did not decide to kill myself

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:28.170
but I'm well acquainted

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:31.924
with the statue of the
author of Paul et Virginie

00:33:31.924 --> 00:33:35.290
the novelist and botanist
Bernardin de Saint-Pierre

00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:37.890
It stands in Paris, the statue,

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.610
in a famous garden

00:33:39.610 --> 00:33:42.063
that he helped create
the Jardin des Plantes.

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:46.750
It was the first public place I went to

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:49.070
in January 2015

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:50.790
with my family and friends

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:53.870
when I was living in the
Pitie salpetriere hospital

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:55.650
and preparing myself

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:58.010
from one operation to the next

00:33:58.010 --> 00:33:59.460
for the graft of the fibula

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:01.430
to replace my jawbone.

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:05.050
On that afternoon I had a
large bandage on my face,

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:06.430
a cap on my head,

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:10.010
an overcoat that was too
long and too big for me

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:11.690
and on my shoulder

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:15.910
a kind of handbag which
with the help of a tube

00:34:15.910 --> 00:34:18.030
sucked up the pews and her oddities

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:20.490
that were coming out
of the hole in my jaw,

00:34:20.490 --> 00:34:21.323
the VACS,

00:34:21.323 --> 00:34:26.260
Vacuum Assisted Closure System.

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.740
My brother took a few photos of me

00:34:29.740 --> 00:34:32.820
lost in this overcoat and bandage.

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:34.880
I looked like a ghost.

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:36.180
At this moment,

00:34:36.180 --> 00:34:38.050
I too sad I'm a sad person.

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:40.010
Half-way between life

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:42.450
and the imagination of life.

00:34:42.450 --> 00:34:44.930
But an imagination that is determined

00:34:44.930 --> 00:34:49.670
by the death that this
life has just gone through.

00:34:49.670 --> 00:34:51.700
By the deaths that at that time

00:34:51.700 --> 00:34:54.670
I found so hard to drive away.

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:55.833
I am floating.

00:34:56.950 --> 00:35:01.160
I was the one who asked to visit
this garden of my childhood

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:02.850
quite close to the hospital

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:06.080
where I had to return
to a few hours later.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:10.530
I wanted above all to
move as slowly as possible

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:14.073
through the greenhouses
where the cactuses are.

00:35:15.050 --> 00:35:16.920
I have always liked cactuses

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:19.500
and the warmth of the greenhouses

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:21.420
in which they are found.

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:23.240
when I'm in these places

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:25.540
I feel like I'm eternal

00:35:25.540 --> 00:35:28.083
that nothing could
happen to me ever again.

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:31.120
On the way to the greenhouses,

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:33.100
I stopped in front of the statue

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:35.180
of Bernardin de Saint-Pierre.

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:38.700
It is located a few
dozen metre from the zoo

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:41.250
where I also went when I was a child

00:35:41.250 --> 00:35:44.720
and which the learned writer founded.

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:46.760
I look at the statue with pleasure

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:48.610
almost with gratitude.

00:35:48.610 --> 00:35:49.570
It did me good.

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:50.403
Why?

00:35:50.403 --> 00:35:53.870
In addition to his beauty
it has one particularity.

00:35:53.870 --> 00:35:54.823
Two levels.

00:35:55.890 --> 00:35:57.880
Set on this on his base,

00:35:57.880 --> 00:35:59.760
his chin on his hand,

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:02.430
the writer seems to be dreaming

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:06.261
like a melancholic God about his creatures

00:36:06.261 --> 00:36:11.160
which are scattered in the
battled earth beneath Him.

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:12.430
He dominates them

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:14.690
because he imagines them.

00:36:14.690 --> 00:36:17.790
But is this imagination
leads him elsewhere

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:20.320
toward an idyllic and tragic world.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:22.370
This world ends in fact

00:36:22.370 --> 00:36:25.030
with separation solitude and death.

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:27.120
Back on the island of Mauritius,

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:31.763
Virginia drowns in a shipwreck
and Paul dies of sorrow.

00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:35.050
Paul and Virginia was an essential book

00:36:35.050 --> 00:36:37.570
for generation of readers.

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:40.833
It made them imagine
and love tragic person.

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:44.590
It made them believe
what they were probably

00:36:44.590 --> 00:36:45.920
ready to believe

00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:49.460
that true love is an
impossibility and a tragedy.

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:52.100
And this impossibility and tragedy

00:36:52.100 --> 00:36:56.420
were precisely what fed the
desire to experience that love

00:36:56.420 --> 00:37:00.350
to experience it all the way to the end.

00:37:00.350 --> 00:37:03.460
Paul in Virginia is
certainly a feel-good book.

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:05.270
There was a genuine enjoyment

00:37:05.270 --> 00:37:07.490
in experiencing desperate love.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:12.450
In soaking one's heart in
an exotic and morbid liquor.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:14.620
Enjoyment is not as you know,

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:18.130
necessarily incompatible
with masochistic reverie.

00:37:18.130 --> 00:37:20.730
I looked with gratitude at the statue

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:23.010
of Bernardin de Saint-Pierre this day

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:25.750
and his infortunate creatures.

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:28.230
Literature could include the situation

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:30.130
that I was reading through

00:37:30.130 --> 00:37:34.580
and inspire the feelings that
I had decided to experience.

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:38.920
It could above all led me to explore them.

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:42.130
I left Paul and Virginia to their sad fate

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:44.850
in order to return to the hospital

00:37:44.850 --> 00:37:48.380
and after visiting the
greenhouse with the cactuses.

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:50.763
To Death Valley and Sergeant Tyree.

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:54.760
I come now to two final questions.

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:55.860
The evening of the day

00:37:55.860 --> 00:37:57.950
when the magazine asked me for the list

00:37:57.950 --> 00:37:59.690
of my feel-good books

00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:02.737
I said to my wife with annoyance,

00:38:02.737 --> 00:38:04.617
"Why are they asking me that.

00:38:04.617 --> 00:38:06.060
"Why me?"

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:08.247
She looked at me surprised and replied,

00:38:08.247 --> 00:38:12.007
"Well because you wrote a
feel-good book. Didn't you?

00:38:12.007 --> 00:38:13.947
"You may not have wanted to write it

00:38:13.947 --> 00:38:16.557
"but for most of your readers,

00:38:16.557 --> 00:38:18.197
"that's what you did.

00:38:18.197 --> 00:38:19.837
"Whether you liked it or not,

00:38:19.837 --> 00:38:21.157
"you did them good.

00:38:21.157 --> 00:38:23.507
"At least that's what they'll tell you."

00:38:24.730 --> 00:38:26.177
Still more annoyed I said,

00:38:26.177 --> 00:38:28.497
"But I didn't give any receipts

00:38:28.497 --> 00:38:30.530
"and there is no happy end at all.

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:31.363
On the contrary,

00:38:31.363 --> 00:38:35.070
the book ends on November 13th 2015

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:37.180
the day of the attack of the Bataclan.

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:38.560
I was in Manhattan.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:40.530
I could hardly stand.

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:41.620
I could hardly stand up,

00:38:41.620 --> 00:38:43.890
I had the feeling that the
ghosts of the Twin Towers

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:45.730
were rising to collapse again.

00:38:45.730 --> 00:38:49.070
I no longer knew what
world I was like living in.

00:38:49.070 --> 00:38:52.330
My surgical reconstruction
was far from finished

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:55.430
and as if that were not enough

00:38:55.430 --> 00:38:57.970
I left the last word to my surgeon

00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:01.400
who told me to put off by return to France

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:02.663
as long as I could.

00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:06.103
My wife replied still more calmly,

00:39:07.067 --> 00:39:09.067
"It's not a happy end

00:39:09.067 --> 00:39:12.547
"but it tells the story of
a successful reconstruction

00:39:12.547 --> 00:39:15.497
"of a battle that you waged won.

00:39:15.497 --> 00:39:18.727
"you didn't need to give
receives for people to feed them.

00:39:18.727 --> 00:39:20.657
"On the contrary it's precisely

00:39:20.657 --> 00:39:22.417
"because you didn't give any

00:39:22.417 --> 00:39:23.797
"that you did them good."

00:39:25.430 --> 00:39:26.610
I thought about the letters

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.000
I had recived

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:29.750
that I was still receiving.

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:31.660
There were a hundred of them.

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:34.200
They now fill two whole boxes,

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:35.180
one in the cellar

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:37.460
the other under my bed.

00:39:37.460 --> 00:39:39.870
I opened the boxes and we read

00:39:39.870 --> 00:39:41.343
what had been written to me.

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:44.620
Most of the letters came from patients,

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:45.870
former patients,

00:39:45.870 --> 00:39:46.820
caregivers,

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:48.690
former caregivers.

00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:49.820
In a country like France

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:52.210
where the medical system is still strong

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:54.020
and socially so important,

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:55.933
that includes a lot of people.

00:39:56.860 --> 00:40:00.430
One patient had given my
first name to the drip stand

00:40:00.430 --> 00:40:02.130
with which she moved

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:04.433
all around the halls of her Hospital.

00:40:05.580 --> 00:40:09.520
Women gave my book to their
sick children or husbands

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:14.090
so that it might accompanies
them like a Vietcong.

00:40:14.090 --> 00:40:16.560
And when the children or husband died,

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:19.570
they reread the book to find them alive.

00:40:19.570 --> 00:40:20.950
You might perhaps ask,

00:40:20.950 --> 00:40:23.400
why is he talking on you
about female readers?

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:24.820
The answer is simple,

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:27.710
it is primarily women who were reading

00:40:27.710 --> 00:40:29.020
at least in France

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:31.153
and in any case have read my book.

00:40:33.670 --> 00:40:36.620
In the world where I spent three months

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:38.510
I also learned that many patients

00:40:38.510 --> 00:40:41.840
now had my book on their bedside tables.

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:46.210
My surgeon told me that
she sensed in this demand

00:40:46.210 --> 00:40:47.507
even complain,

00:40:47.507 --> 00:40:51.120
"Take care of us the way
you took care of him."

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:52.640
And to tell the truth,

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:53.670
I understood them

00:40:53.670 --> 00:40:55.980
because I had always thought

00:40:55.980 --> 00:41:00.980
that the attention given me
should have been the rule

00:41:01.130 --> 00:41:04.000
and not the exception of the system.

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:07.940
That the patients were most
held out my book to my surgeon.

00:41:07.940 --> 00:41:10.360
Who was I addressing then?

00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:11.800
The person she was

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:14.650
or the character on paper she had become?

00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:17.300
One reader also sent me the diary

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:19.810
he would I would kept at the time

00:41:19.810 --> 00:41:21.960
that he was reading Disturbance.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:24.240
Nothing about his family life

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:27.980
and his private life
was Eden from from me.

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:29.820
I learned everything about his wife,

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:30.653
his children,

00:41:30.653 --> 00:41:31.710
their habits,

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:33.930
desires and conflicts.

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:36.440
I accompanied them even to bed

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:38.180
and in their lovemaking.

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:42.190
Sentences I had written
appeared very regularly.

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:44.170
They had entered into his life

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:45.650
and when he had problems

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:47.100
in return to them

00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:48.650
not as solutions

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:49.893
but as crutches.

00:41:51.110 --> 00:41:53.100
I was disturbed by this journal.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:55.960
And more generally by
the excessive intimacy

00:41:55.960 --> 00:41:57.510
of some of the letters

00:41:57.510 --> 00:42:01.530
because so I had written an intimate book.

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:03.530
It seemed to me that I had done

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:05.100
so in such a way

00:42:05.100 --> 00:42:08.193
that the writing itself
put a certain distance.

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:11.580
Everything it brought closer

00:42:11.580 --> 00:42:13.790
to the awareness of readers.

00:42:13.790 --> 00:42:15.520
It was an intimate book

00:42:15.520 --> 00:42:20.520
but I hope at least that it
was not an indiscreet book

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:23.413
nor one that authorized indiscretion.

00:42:24.654 --> 00:42:26.710
Also I did not want to allow readers

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:29.020
to enter my life indiscreetly.

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:33.700
Neither did I want to enter
indiscreetly into theirs.

00:42:33.700 --> 00:42:36.310
Once readers finished the books

00:42:36.310 --> 00:42:39.110
I would have liked them
to say with a slight smile

00:42:40.390 --> 00:42:41.880
like servant Sergeant Tyree

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:43.917
when he returned from Indian Territory

00:42:43.917 --> 00:42:45.163
"I saw this,

00:42:46.787 --> 00:42:48.357
"I experienced that,

00:42:48.357 --> 00:42:50.947
"but so far as commentary is concerned,

00:42:50.947 --> 00:42:53.670
"It ain't my department, sir."

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:57.690
Then I made my little
inventory of the novels

00:42:57.690 --> 00:43:01.040
that in France are connected with illness,

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:01.873
trauma

00:43:01.873 --> 00:43:04.223
and death with medicine.

00:43:05.650 --> 00:43:08.140
There were of course
the novels of narratives

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:10.710
that arose from the first world war.

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:13.600
Many French writers were physicians.

00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:15.360
I suddenly realized

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:16.930
that in the last 10 years

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:18.970
two bestsellers had been set

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:21.636
at least in the world of the hospital.

00:43:21.636 --> 00:43:22.570
D'autres vies que la mienne,

00:43:22.570 --> 00:43:26.135
lives other than my
own by Emmanuel Carrere

00:43:26.135 --> 00:43:27.470
and happily live oh

00:43:27.470 --> 00:43:30.750
Mend the Living, by Maylis de Kerangal

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:32.743
The former is a nonfiction book,

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:35.700
the latter, a novel.

00:43:35.700 --> 00:43:39.010
The first book deals
with a two-fold tragedy.

00:43:39.010 --> 00:43:42.100
That of a family whose
child has been the victim

00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:44.040
of the tsunami in Sri Lanka

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:46.510
at a time when the author was with them.

00:43:46.510 --> 00:43:49.140
And that of a woman
that had a sister-in-law

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:50.940
with dying of cancer.

00:43:50.940 --> 00:43:54.480
What connects the two stories
is a presence of the writer

00:43:54.480 --> 00:43:57.513
an active witness to
these dramatic events.

00:43:58.430 --> 00:44:01.040
The second book becomes
a tragedy of family

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.120
whose child has been in a car accident

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:05.720
that has left him brain dead

00:44:05.720 --> 00:44:10.080
and whose heart is to be
transplanted into another patient.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:11.600
What connects these two families

00:44:11.600 --> 00:44:14.430
experiencing a tragedy is the hospital

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:17.580
and the graft of the dead onto the living.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:20.020
I had read and like both these books

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:22.610
long before the attack on Charlie Hebdo

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:24.860
but it hadn't occurred to me

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:26.453
to reread them afterward.

00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:29.910
I hadn't even thought about them.

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:32.890
Both relate a collective
experience of tragedy.

00:44:32.890 --> 00:44:35.940
They tell how individuals survive death

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:38.050
or survive with death

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:41.080
by struggling and in one way or another

00:44:41.080 --> 00:44:42.810
by coming together.

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:45.340
Then I saw without wishing to

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:47.070
I had done the same thing.

00:44:47.070 --> 00:44:49.330
I had recounted the story of a man

00:44:49.330 --> 00:44:52.460
who was a victim of a terrorist attack

00:44:52.460 --> 00:44:54.520
and who fights to pull through

00:44:54.520 --> 00:44:57.430
but also the story of various
groups of family members,

00:44:57.430 --> 00:45:01.304
friends, co-workers and hospital staff

00:45:01.304 --> 00:45:03.070
whom this attack upset

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:06.770
and were fighting to
help him pull through.

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:10.910
In this sense each of us
had written feel-good books.

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:13.030
What one woman friend of mine

00:45:13.030 --> 00:45:15.730
has called, referring to readers

00:45:15.730 --> 00:45:17.457
of a Emmanuel Carroll's book,

00:45:17.457 --> 00:45:18.840
"Men of goodwill."

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:20.667
Les Hommes de bonne volonte,

00:45:20.667 --> 00:45:24.000
Men of Good Will, was
precisely the title of a

00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:27.090
series of great French humanistic novels

00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:28.370
published in the thirties.

00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:31.133
It was written by a former
physician Jules Romains.

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:35.050
What differentiated my book

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:38.120
from those of Kerangal and carrere was

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:40.930
among other things the point of view.

00:45:40.930 --> 00:45:45.130
The story of Disturbance
is recounted exclusively

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:47.260
from the point of view of the person

00:45:47.260 --> 00:45:49.490
who experienced it.

00:45:49.490 --> 00:45:51.247
I am servant Tyree,

00:45:51.247 --> 00:45:55.800
but I am also the wounded man
he put behind him on his horse

00:45:55.800 --> 00:45:58.940
and whom he saves by giving him first aid

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:01.773
and then by the slow gallop of the text.

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:05.520
A little more than 20 years earlier

00:46:05.520 --> 00:46:08.760
in 1990, another book

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:10.900
that had a great success in France

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:13.410
recounted the story of illnes and death

00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:14.800
connected with AIDS.

00:46:14.800 --> 00:46:17.893
Herve Guibert, To the Friend
Who Did Not Save My Life.

00:46:19.050 --> 00:46:21.310
I had read it when it came out

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:22.890
and it had marked me.

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:24.220
It's a funny, aggressive,

00:46:24.220 --> 00:46:27.350
sly, angry book in which the author

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:30.180
who is like is the patient is constantly

00:46:30.180 --> 00:46:32.620
in a state of mistreat and effective war

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:34.260
against everyone.

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:35.200
His entourage,

00:46:35.200 --> 00:46:36.500
his caregivers.

00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:39.700
He's an individual who
is fighting ungraciously

00:46:39.700 --> 00:46:41.520
and without illusions

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:43.820
to obtain informations and rights

00:46:43.820 --> 00:46:46.530
that are not being given him.

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:49.830
All that he thinks are
not being given to him.

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:54.090
This individual is a product
of a world and a culture

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:55.760
in which the power of the gaze

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:57.400
the presence of the body

00:46:57.400 --> 00:47:00.400
and a certain paranoia
peculiar to intelligence,

00:47:00.400 --> 00:47:01.910
dissolved morality,

00:47:01.910 --> 00:47:03.200
good feelings

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:05.970
and everything that was called humanism.

00:47:05.970 --> 00:47:09.770
Guibert's book was thus in
no way a feel-good book.

00:47:09.770 --> 00:47:11.823
But it made history.

00:47:12.923 --> 00:47:14.350
Carrere and Kerangal's book

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:17.223
along with my own came
out in another time,

00:47:19.030 --> 00:47:20.213
twenty years later.

00:47:21.330 --> 00:47:23.970
I think that in themselves

00:47:23.970 --> 00:47:25.570
they I did not bring any good news

00:47:25.570 --> 00:47:27.970
but perhaps in spite of everything

00:47:27.970 --> 00:47:30.040
they did provide good feelings

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:32.590
that are not found in Guibert's book.

00:47:32.590 --> 00:47:34.340
In those who are marked by a violent

00:47:34.340 --> 00:47:38.260
and transgressive
individualism and by heads

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:42.560
or more exactly they brought
a kind of consolation.

00:47:42.560 --> 00:47:44.750
Consolation is neither healing

00:47:44.750 --> 00:47:46.280
nor illusion

00:47:46.280 --> 00:47:51.280
nor a method intended to make
us better or simply healthy.

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:52.880
It's rather rough caress

00:47:55.978 --> 00:47:58.653
given the misery of the world.

00:47:58.653 --> 00:48:01.370
it's longer requires tongue transgression

00:48:01.370 --> 00:48:04.020
but it does require confidence

00:48:04.020 --> 00:48:06.290
and to a certain extent

00:48:06.290 --> 00:48:08.710
the spectacle of reconciliation

00:48:08.710 --> 00:48:10.790
in order to live in a world

00:48:10.790 --> 00:48:12.673
that provides hardly any at all.

00:48:13.960 --> 00:48:15.703
Is it humanistic caress?

00:48:16.630 --> 00:48:17.760
If it is,

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:20.540
have I really written a feel-good book?

00:48:20.540 --> 00:48:23.500
It is art form in conclusion to reply.

00:48:23.500 --> 00:48:27.060
We are seldom good
analysts of our own books.

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:29.620
I can simply say that at the hospital

00:48:29.620 --> 00:48:32.060
without truly thinking about it,

00:48:32.060 --> 00:48:35.620
I made Proust's, Kafka's and Mann's books.

00:48:35.620 --> 00:48:37.320
My feel-good books.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:40.600
Books that were returning from unknown,

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:41.433
threatening,

00:48:41.433 --> 00:48:42.400
marvelous

00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:44.555
and hostile lands.

00:48:44.555 --> 00:48:47.730
They didn't hide from me

00:48:47.730 --> 00:48:49.510
anything they had seen.

00:48:49.510 --> 00:48:50.900
But better yet,

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:55.470
they didn't hide from me
anything that I experienced.

00:48:55.470 --> 00:48:56.870
And they have rejoined

00:48:56.870 --> 00:48:58.600
without commentary

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:00.350
and with a slight smile

00:49:00.350 --> 00:49:02.880
that endless territory, my library.

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:03.921
Thank you.

00:49:03.921 --> 00:49:07.088
(audience applauding)

00:49:29.462 --> 00:49:30.295
- Thank You so much.

00:49:30.295 --> 00:49:32.734
We have a little time for some questions.

00:49:32.734 --> 00:49:33.944
And the microphone,

00:49:33.944 --> 00:49:35.301
come down with the microphone.

00:49:35.301 --> 00:49:36.494
We would like to have a few questions.

00:49:36.494 --> 00:49:38.994
Please, don't leave the stage.

00:49:47.044 --> 00:49:48.620
- [Alice] Thank you Philippe.

00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:51.230
It's my pleasure to break the ice.

00:49:51.230 --> 00:49:52.387
I have two questions really

00:49:52.387 --> 00:49:53.530
and one is,

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:57.200
why do you think it's
women who are reading now?

00:49:57.200 --> 00:49:59.340
And the second question may be related.

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:03.150
It's, you're setting up some boundaries

00:50:03.150 --> 00:50:06.090
between confessional literature

00:50:06.090 --> 00:50:10.610
and a kind of discreet
but intimate literature.

00:50:10.610 --> 00:50:13.363
And I wonder how you
conceive of that intimacy.

00:50:16.460 --> 00:50:17.453
- Thank you Alice.

00:50:21.539 --> 00:50:23.460
The first question

00:50:24.312 --> 00:50:28.200
I don't know because I'm not
a sociologist of literature

00:50:29.506 --> 00:50:33.697
and you know in Paris
there is a big subway.

00:50:34.674 --> 00:50:36.220
I always take the subway.

00:50:36.220 --> 00:50:37.790
And it's a subway as in a train.

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:40.610
You can see that at least in Paris,

00:50:40.610 --> 00:50:41.983
who is reading a book?

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:45.143
A woman, most of all.

00:50:51.560 --> 00:50:52.690
It's very difficult now

00:50:52.690 --> 00:50:53.523
because

00:50:56.810 --> 00:50:57.670
I think

00:50:59.740 --> 00:51:01.630
we will see later

00:51:01.630 --> 00:51:04.890
when the relationship
between men and women change

00:51:04.890 --> 00:51:08.420
into the world if it's going to change.

00:51:08.420 --> 00:51:11.398
But as far as I know,

00:51:11.398 --> 00:51:13.930
I'm a man of my age, 56.

00:51:13.930 --> 00:51:17.870
I think that many women

00:51:17.870 --> 00:51:19.905
of my generation I knew

00:51:19.905 --> 00:51:21.037
they have

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:26.800
more access to interior life

00:51:29.860 --> 00:51:34.860
because they had less
access to public life

00:51:36.000 --> 00:51:39.450
and so they had this interiority

00:51:41.410 --> 00:51:46.093
led them to be more sensible
to the stories I read.

00:51:47.460 --> 00:51:50.300
It is a very desperately
classical response

00:51:50.300 --> 00:51:51.610
may be old-fashioned

00:51:51.610 --> 00:51:55.740
but that's why I would
be very curious to know

00:51:55.740 --> 00:51:59.120
what will happen in the
regeneration coming.

00:51:59.120 --> 00:52:02.760
I hope of course just as men as women

00:52:02.760 --> 00:52:03.593
would read,

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:08.410
you go out all the time

00:52:08.410 --> 00:52:09.790
I can check now

00:52:12.773 --> 00:52:14.670
it is still the women who are reading

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:16.253
at least in the public spaces.

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:20.020
And the second question

00:52:20.020 --> 00:52:21.820
I'm not sure I understood well.

00:52:21.820 --> 00:52:24.937
The separation between nonfiction and,

00:52:24.937 --> 00:52:27.520
(lady mumbles)

00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:42.150
I think the only answer can be personal

00:52:42.150 --> 00:52:44.593
and the fact is as a reader,

00:52:47.810 --> 00:52:50.590
I prefer intimate tales

00:52:51.730 --> 00:52:55.330
and I don't care if they're
fiction or nonfiction.

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:59.373
I mean last summer I
was reading Middlematch.

00:53:00.780 --> 00:53:05.780
and of course I loved it.

00:53:05.900 --> 00:53:09.120
And for me I don't make any difference

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:10.407
between Middlematch

00:53:10.407 --> 00:53:15.407
and the great personal
nonfiction book, you know.

00:53:19.260 --> 00:53:20.370
These are characters

00:53:21.464 --> 00:53:23.453
and I actually I wrote my book.

00:53:24.380 --> 00:53:25.930
Everything is real in a sense

00:53:25.930 --> 00:53:29.900
that it is everything I experienced.

00:53:29.900 --> 00:53:31.613
And there is no lie at all.

00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:39.710
And I don't write in this book a sentence

00:53:39.910 --> 00:53:42.400
about things I didn't directly experience.

00:53:42.400 --> 00:53:46.310
But I composed it as a novel

00:53:46.310 --> 00:53:49.250
in the sense that it is
a tale with characters.

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:52.550
And I was speaking in my conference

00:53:52.550 --> 00:53:53.933
about my surgeon Chloe.

00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:58.333
She is now a very famous
character in in France.

00:54:00.090 --> 00:54:03.080
Interviewed on TV and radio and so on.

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:06.610
And it is very strange for her

00:54:06.610 --> 00:54:07.443
more than for me

00:54:07.443 --> 00:54:09.903
because, who is she?

00:54:11.340 --> 00:54:16.340
Is she the character I created in my book

00:54:17.060 --> 00:54:19.230
which is only from my point of view?

00:54:19.230 --> 00:54:21.677
Of course when she asked she says,

00:54:21.677 --> 00:54:22.827
"No.

00:54:22.827 --> 00:54:24.657
"I'm another person.

00:54:24.657 --> 00:54:27.050
"I'm not the person he describes."

00:54:27.050 --> 00:54:28.890
But on the other way

00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:32.990
she knows she is this person

00:54:32.990 --> 00:54:37.990
and now she will be as
far as the books arrive.

00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:39.850
She is this person.

00:54:39.850 --> 00:54:42.600
So she's the character

00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:46.070
and this is the special
part of the nonfiction.

00:54:46.070 --> 00:54:47.050
she is the character

00:54:47.050 --> 00:54:48.810
and she's a real person.

00:54:48.810 --> 00:54:52.940
And I can tell you it's very strange to,

00:54:52.940 --> 00:54:56.290
and from time to time we are together

00:54:56.290 --> 00:54:58.713
in a kind of show.

00:54:59.740 --> 00:55:04.740
And I never know to whom I'm speaking to.

00:55:08.613 --> 00:55:10.630
And who is answering

00:55:10.630 --> 00:55:15.630
and because now, of course
she still my surgeon

00:55:16.450 --> 00:55:19.567
so she's supposed to work on me

00:55:22.600 --> 00:55:26.230
in the months coming again, a bit.

00:55:26.230 --> 00:55:30.480
And so, of course I have
to kill the character

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:33.083
to go back to the surgeon.

00:55:35.890 --> 00:55:37.660
And she has to kill the writer

00:55:37.660 --> 00:55:38.973
to go back to the patient.

00:55:43.830 --> 00:55:45.630
- [Man] Yes, thanks so much

00:55:45.630 --> 00:55:49.140
for sharing your story with us

00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:50.733
for the inspirational talk.

00:55:51.885 --> 00:55:56.330
Can we now define feeling good

00:55:56.330 --> 00:55:57.220
or feel good

00:55:58.312 --> 00:56:03.312
as the struggle of recognizing adversity,

00:56:07.130 --> 00:56:09.820
facing it head-on

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:12.523
and working together in community.

00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:18.850
Doctors, nurses, physical therapists,

00:56:18.850 --> 00:56:23.170
friends and family to overcome it

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:26.820
and triumph you know,

00:56:26.820 --> 00:56:29.963
one adversity at a time.

00:56:31.160 --> 00:56:33.823
Is this what you're trying to define?

00:56:34.850 --> 00:56:35.683
- Yes.

00:56:35.683 --> 00:56:36.516
I think,

00:56:37.414 --> 00:56:38.363
in a way Yes.

00:56:39.870 --> 00:56:40.971
Feel-good is that

00:56:40.971 --> 00:56:44.500
I mean what is important is

00:56:45.640 --> 00:56:48.230
I told that I hope at
the end of the conference

00:56:48.230 --> 00:56:50.770
is that the fact that a group,

00:56:50.770 --> 00:56:52.260
different group of persons

00:56:54.070 --> 00:56:56.210
not only helped me to recover

00:56:56.210 --> 00:56:58.600
help the victim that it was me,

00:56:58.600 --> 00:56:59.800
it could be another one.

00:57:00.636 --> 00:57:02.090
But they helped themselves

00:57:02.090 --> 00:57:04.350
helping the victim.

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:07.860
And that helping means they think,

00:57:07.860 --> 00:57:09.210
they act,

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:10.940
they talk to each other

00:57:10.940 --> 00:57:13.000
and they change.

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:15.890
And by doing all that,

00:57:15.890 --> 00:57:18.130
life is coming back to a place

00:57:18.130 --> 00:57:21.003
where death was invited.

00:57:21.890 --> 00:57:22.723
And

00:57:25.930 --> 00:57:26.860
if I am reluctant

00:57:26.860 --> 00:57:29.540
to the idea of feel-good book and so on

00:57:29.540 --> 00:57:30.373
is because

00:57:31.230 --> 00:57:33.200
you can never generalize

00:57:33.200 --> 00:57:34.970
and treat as a writer to,

00:57:34.970 --> 00:57:37.450
you can't generalize an experience.

00:57:37.450 --> 00:57:39.270
You can tell an experience

00:57:39.270 --> 00:57:42.234
but it's very difficult to think

00:57:42.234 --> 00:57:46.810
that what works for me

00:57:46.810 --> 00:57:47.950
will work for you

00:57:47.950 --> 00:57:49.053
or for another one.

00:57:51.380 --> 00:57:53.300
And as a writer however

00:57:53.300 --> 00:57:55.487
and the great reader of novel,

00:57:55.487 --> 00:57:58.457
the novel is a great novel it is really,

00:58:00.657 --> 00:58:04.230
always the story of a group of person

00:58:04.230 --> 00:58:05.810
and a group of person in a society

00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:08.910
but these are individuals.

00:58:08.910 --> 00:58:13.050
The great novelists won't
give you any receives

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:16.060
of the way how to recover and so on.

00:58:16.060 --> 00:58:17.950
He tells you,

00:58:17.950 --> 00:58:19.870
this guy in recover this way

00:58:19.870 --> 00:58:20.850
and it tells a story

00:58:20.850 --> 00:58:22.237
and this guy he failed.

00:58:23.158 --> 00:58:28.000
And this is a reader
one who experienced this

00:58:31.644 --> 00:58:34.244
and with this after a while

00:58:34.244 --> 00:58:37.000
to think about his own life

00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:42.000
but you won't find any
solution in the book.

00:58:46.967 --> 00:58:48.033
There is no solution.

00:58:49.430 --> 00:58:50.510
There is a solution

00:58:50.510 --> 00:58:52.660
but only in one life

00:58:52.660 --> 00:58:54.223
which is not another life.

00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:56.750
- [Gary] Thank you.

00:59:00.420 --> 00:59:01.890
- Maybe it sounds pessimistic.

00:59:01.890 --> 00:59:04.013
For me it's not pessimistic at all.

00:59:04.013 --> 00:59:05.160
It's optimistic.

00:59:05.160 --> 00:59:10.160
It means we are free

00:59:10.370 --> 00:59:12.240
to read whatever we want to read

00:59:12.240 --> 00:59:15.320
and then we are free to find our way in

00:59:19.060 --> 00:59:20.950
what happens to us

00:59:20.950 --> 00:59:24.850
in a certain limits, of course

00:59:24.850 --> 00:59:29.560
because when the disasters
are falling on us

00:59:30.650 --> 00:59:32.650
it's very difficult to feel free.

00:59:32.650 --> 00:59:34.440
But then you can fight

00:59:34.440 --> 00:59:36.310
and find your own way

00:59:36.310 --> 00:59:37.513
to feel free.

00:59:39.180 --> 00:59:43.263
I found my own way
strangely in the hospital.

00:59:45.250 --> 00:59:47.480
It was very very tough

00:59:47.480 --> 00:59:48.640
but I like the hospital

00:59:48.640 --> 00:59:50.993
because for me,

00:59:53.140 --> 00:59:55.833
I think I was at my best
when I was in the hospital.

00:59:57.030 --> 00:59:58.750
It is it may be very strange

00:59:58.750 --> 01:00:01.770
but I can assure you it was a moment

01:00:01.770 --> 01:00:06.040
I had the best I can do

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:08.680
not only for me but for others.

01:00:08.680 --> 01:00:09.513
I think I gave it

01:00:09.513 --> 01:00:11.763
when I was in the hospital.

01:00:14.470 --> 01:00:17.770
And then I tried to give it again

01:00:17.770 --> 01:00:19.800
in another way like writing.

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:22.950
But that writing is my is my job

01:00:22.950 --> 01:00:24.600
since 35 years

01:00:24.600 --> 01:00:25.513
so it's different.

01:00:31.725 --> 01:00:33.810
- [Gary] I will address a question to you

01:00:35.560 --> 01:00:38.260
that is coming a little bit
out of left field perhaps

01:00:39.527 --> 01:00:43.100
but we were talking in my
office before you spoke

01:00:43.100 --> 01:00:45.470
about the importance of
music in your recovery

01:00:45.470 --> 01:00:46.790
and your listening to music

01:00:46.790 --> 01:00:48.160
and somebody giving you a gift

01:00:48.160 --> 01:00:48.993
of well, you,

01:00:48.993 --> 01:00:50.780
It was Bach in particular
that you listen to

01:00:50.780 --> 01:00:52.570
somebody gave you a gift of

01:00:52.570 --> 01:00:54.360
over 200 cantatas of Bach

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:55.920
that's a lot of listening.

01:00:55.920 --> 01:00:58.490
And I'm wondering if
somebody had approached you

01:00:58.490 --> 01:00:59.967
and asked you,

01:00:59.967 --> 01:01:01.877
"Sent me a list of feel-good music."

01:01:02.800 --> 01:01:05.390
Would you have felt the same ambivalences

01:01:05.390 --> 01:01:08.230
and even anger about such a question?

01:01:08.230 --> 01:01:09.927
- It's a good question

01:01:09.927 --> 01:01:13.380
and we usually say it's a good question

01:01:13.380 --> 01:01:14.730
when we have no the answer.

01:01:17.351 --> 01:01:18.951
But I'll try to find the answer.

01:01:21.490 --> 01:01:22.540
Let's take an example

01:01:22.540 --> 01:01:24.900
let's think about the one
was very important to me

01:01:24.900 --> 01:01:28.030
when I was precisely
in the hospital, Bach.

01:01:28.030 --> 01:01:30.283
Because it was like a ritual for me.

01:01:32.453 --> 01:01:33.720
I tell about that in the book.

01:01:33.720 --> 01:01:35.410
I would listen to Bach,

01:01:35.410 --> 01:01:38.211
most of all how do you say in English

01:01:38.211 --> 01:01:40.050
(foreign language)

01:01:40.050 --> 01:01:41.450
A piano

01:01:43.680 --> 01:01:45.103
and several versions.

01:01:46.570 --> 01:01:48.153
So was it a feel-good music?

01:01:51.680 --> 01:01:52.970
I don't believe in God

01:01:52.970 --> 01:01:55.660
but Bach

01:01:57.140 --> 01:01:59.410
made me doubt not about the fact

01:01:59.410 --> 01:02:00.330
that God would exist

01:02:00.330 --> 01:02:02.240
but that something existed

01:02:02.240 --> 01:02:05.020
because I was really feeling

01:02:05.020 --> 01:02:09.120
that he was through his music

01:02:09.120 --> 01:02:14.120
kind of peace was falling in my room.

01:02:14.840 --> 01:02:18.830
And not on me in my room
but in in the block too

01:02:18.830 --> 01:02:20.360
when I was going to the block

01:02:20.360 --> 01:02:23.050
because what happened is step by step

01:02:25.280 --> 01:02:27.120
people from the hospital

01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:30.960
I mean carer, surgeons, nurses

01:02:30.960 --> 01:02:32.430
they would listen to Bach too

01:02:33.300 --> 01:02:37.230
and I tell one that I write to the block

01:02:38.900 --> 01:02:43.010
and these days it wasn't
my Chloe my surgeon

01:02:43.010 --> 01:02:45.270
but another surgeon

01:02:45.270 --> 01:02:46.940
who is now a friend actually

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:51.050
and he put some Bach

01:02:51.050 --> 01:02:53.340
before he made me sleep

01:02:54.470 --> 01:02:56.003
in the block so I was really in the,

01:02:56.003 --> 01:02:57.920
before the operation.

01:02:57.920 --> 01:02:59.337
And he told me,

01:02:59.337 --> 01:03:02.147
"I guess you don't know
this interpretation."

01:03:02.147 --> 01:03:05.430
And so he had looked for him

01:03:05.430 --> 01:03:07.340
and so it was a kind of

01:03:10.301 --> 01:03:12.390
feel-good music for all of us

01:03:13.760 --> 01:03:16.710
but as it that sense to say

01:03:16.710 --> 01:03:18.810
that Bach is a feel-good music.

01:03:18.810 --> 01:03:20.711
Is much more than that

01:03:20.711 --> 01:03:23.610
because on another way it tells you

01:03:26.180 --> 01:03:28.837
doesn't tell you makes you feel that

01:03:34.690 --> 01:03:37.080
even if you are suffering a lot

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:41.360
and you are absolutely
entitled to complain whatsoever

01:03:41.360 --> 01:03:43.640
there is something in
this music that tells you

01:03:43.640 --> 01:03:47.990
that the best you can do is

01:03:47.990 --> 01:03:49.863
to be silent,

01:03:50.770 --> 01:03:52.370
to be quiet.

01:03:52.370 --> 01:03:55.653
And because there is something stronger,

01:03:59.380 --> 01:04:01.983
deeper coming from I don't know where.

01:04:03.010 --> 01:04:04.630
And will pass who is music

01:04:08.432 --> 01:04:10.680
and I could really feel this

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:14.150
because it's unusual to listen to Bach

01:04:14.150 --> 01:04:15.713
in such a hospital.

01:04:18.560 --> 01:04:20.283
People usually they watch TV.

01:04:21.920 --> 01:04:23.023
And not good TV.

01:04:24.450 --> 01:04:27.090
And I understand perfectly
why they do that.

01:04:27.090 --> 01:04:29.933
It's like a drug.

01:04:30.850 --> 01:04:34.723
And like do people in jail.

01:04:35.810 --> 01:04:38.380
Of course I think it's a very bad drug

01:04:38.380 --> 01:04:40.880
will put you

01:04:40.880 --> 01:04:42.490
to depression

01:04:42.490 --> 01:04:44.810
but Bach was calm

01:04:44.810 --> 01:04:48.200
and so even it was a way for me

01:04:48.200 --> 01:04:49.773
to attract,

01:04:50.990 --> 01:04:54.733
to bring the nurses and
surggeon to my place.

01:04:55.710 --> 01:04:56.840
They would like my place

01:04:56.840 --> 01:04:59.193
because there was this music.

01:05:00.250 --> 01:05:02.903
So it was feel-good music for them too.

01:05:07.520 --> 01:05:09.160
- [Alice] I was wondering
if you could speak

01:05:09.160 --> 01:05:10.620
to the topic of satire.

01:05:10.620 --> 01:05:11.800
- To the?

01:05:11.800 --> 01:05:14.000
- [Alice] To the topic of satire

01:05:14.000 --> 01:05:15.960
and whether or not that has a place

01:05:15.960 --> 01:05:17.090
in a feel-good book

01:05:17.090 --> 01:05:19.210
or as part of your recovery

01:05:19.210 --> 01:05:21.480
or just if your relationship to satire

01:05:21.480 --> 01:05:23.780
in general has changed?

01:05:23.780 --> 01:05:25.817
- I don't understand the word satire.

01:05:25.817 --> 01:05:27.640
Satere or to satire?

01:05:27.640 --> 01:05:28.580
Oh yes. Okay.

01:05:28.580 --> 01:05:29.413
Satire.

01:05:30.600 --> 01:05:33.290
I take advantage of you to forgive you

01:05:33.290 --> 01:05:35.383
for my very bad pronunciation.

01:05:42.365 --> 01:05:43.450
As you know

01:05:43.450 --> 01:05:46.373
I'm still working in
this satirical newspaper,

01:05:46.373 --> 01:05:47.920
Charlie Hebdo.

01:05:47.920 --> 01:05:51.420
And so this satirical newspaper is

01:05:52.570 --> 01:05:55.770
most of all a newspaper of cartoonists.

01:05:55.770 --> 01:05:57.595
And I'm not a cartoonist.

01:05:57.595 --> 01:05:59.293
But I used to know them,

01:05:59.293 --> 01:06:01.460
to be with them,

01:06:01.460 --> 01:06:02.803
and to live in their mood.

01:06:03.770 --> 01:06:04.603
So,

01:06:05.880 --> 01:06:07.400
I suppose if I decided

01:06:08.840 --> 01:06:12.300
to work for them 15 years ago

01:06:12.300 --> 01:06:16.690
It is maybe because the satire was part of

01:06:16.690 --> 01:06:18.510
what I like in life

01:06:18.510 --> 01:06:20.660
because I think simply its for me.

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:22.740
and then what I say it's always for me

01:06:22.740 --> 01:06:24.900
because I think

01:06:26.979 --> 01:06:27.890
it helps me

01:06:29.510 --> 01:06:33.163
to face problems and disasters.

01:06:34.010 --> 01:06:38.140
And I think really it
help people from Charlie

01:06:38.140 --> 01:06:40.363
to face these disasters,

01:06:41.480 --> 01:06:42.410
this massacre.

01:06:42.410 --> 01:06:45.120
I mean the survivors, of course.

01:06:45.120 --> 01:06:50.000
And because we always made fun.

01:06:50.000 --> 01:06:51.430
Even in the hospital,

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:53.370
I was with wounded friends

01:06:53.370 --> 01:06:56.390
and we always made fun
about what happened to us

01:06:57.330 --> 01:07:00.510
and about us as patients.

01:07:00.510 --> 01:07:01.660
Bad jokes,

01:07:01.660 --> 01:07:04.220
I can tell you because they were very bad.

01:07:04.220 --> 01:07:07.653
And this was a therapy.

01:07:08.780 --> 01:07:10.560
The book is not at all a therapy.

01:07:10.560 --> 01:07:12.630
The book is a creation

01:07:12.630 --> 01:07:16.620
that this kind of bad jokes we had,

01:07:16.620 --> 01:07:20.910
this presence of satire was very important

01:07:20.910 --> 01:07:23.410
because precisely,

01:07:23.410 --> 01:07:26.950
satire is to laugh

01:07:26.950 --> 01:07:28.290
about what is not funny

01:07:29.900 --> 01:07:31.826
and to turn it in a way

01:07:31.826 --> 01:07:33.076
makes it funny.

01:07:34.221 --> 01:07:36.388
That is a sense of satire.

01:07:53.060 --> 01:07:57.060
(Audience applauding)
Thank you.

01:08:05.345 --> 01:08:07.928
(gentle music)

